## Summary

I refactored `rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs` to look like Rust rather than a thin transliteration of C. The biggest change was promoting every free function whose first argument was `&[mut] Quic` or `&[mut] Cnx` to inherent methods on those types — about 28 functions, organized into multiple `impl Quic` and `impl Cnx` blocks that preserve the original section structure (`init_master_tls_context`, `Cnx::process_tls_stream`, `Quic::tls_time`, `Quic::seed_public_random`, `Cnx::app_secret`, `Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`, etc.). I kept the `tls_` prefix on the three TLS setters that have a same-named public-API wrapper in `lib.rs`/`picoquic.h` (`tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`, `tls_set_client_authentication`, `tls_set_use_exporter`) and added a docstring section explaining the public/internal split, since the wrappers without `tls_` already exist on `Quic`. `crypto_context_free` became `CryptoContext::free_handles` with a note explaining why it isn't a `Drop` (it runs on key rotation, not on parent teardown). On the error-model side, `aead_decrypt_generic`/`aead_decrypt_mp` now return `Result<usize, Error>` with the C `SIZE_MAX` sentinel mapped to `Err`; `Cnx::process_tls_stream` switched from `Result<i32, Error>` to `Result<usize, Error>` since data-consumed counts are non-negative; `verify_retry_protection` now returns the new length as `Result<usize, Error>` rather than mutating `&mut usize`. Renamed the `tlscontext_free` free function to `tls_context_free` for snake-case consistency. Free functions that operate on opaque tls handles, on no receiver (global RNG, hash factories, cipher-suite lookups, init/unload), or on a non-quic primary argument (`setup_initial_master_secret`, `rotate_app_secret`, `setup_test_aead_context`) stayed as free functions per the existing pointer-shape policy. Updated the module-level docs to describe the new shape conventions and the public/internal naming split. `cargo check` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` both pass clean.


